From 4aa7e374277ad44bba8de228561fdb0b67162604 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:45:11 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] workspace, settings_ui: order the status bar's panel buttons
 in the settings window
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.36 (ask 59), second half. Upstream orders each dock's
buttons by the panels' fixed activation_priority, and a button changes
side only with its panel's dock (right-click → Dock Left/Right/Bottom).
The operator asked to order them from the settings window -- explicitly
not by drag and drop, which would live in this crate's most-changed code
and be rebased on every bump.

- `status_bar.panel_button_order` (default []) lists panel persistent
  names. workspace::panel_button_order::order_by_setting puts the listed
  panels first, in the listed order, and keeps everything else where
  upstream put it; a name listed twice or carried by no panel changes
  nothing and no button is ever dropped. PanelButtons::render tags each
  button with its panel's name and applies it after upstream reverses
  the right dock, so the order reads left to right as shown.
- Settings → Window & Layout → Status Bar → "Panel Button Order" is a
  sub-page (settings_ui/src/pages/panel_button_order_page.rs) listing the
  panels of the window the settings were opened from, by side, with up
  and down arrows (disabled at the ends) and "Reset to Default". Each move
  writes the whole order (move_panel), so the file always says exactly
  what is shown. Dock gains panel_names() for it. Labels split the
  persistent name ("GitPanel" → "Git Panel").
- The VS Code import, which builds StatusBarSettingsContent field by
  field, leaves the new field None.

Known limit: the page lists every panel of a dock, including one that
draws no button (a hidden panel); moving it changes nothing visible.
Telling them apart needs each panel's icon(), which takes the original
window's context.

Tests: panel_button_order::tests (6: no setting changes nothing; listed
first in order; unknown and repeated names ignored, nothing lost; a move
swaps neighbours; moves stop at their side's ends; labels) and
panel_button_order_page::tests (2: groups follow the status bar and skip
empty sides; the setting applies per side). Seen failing with the
setting ignored (3 ordering tests) and with the bounds check removed
(the ends test), each probe alone. Xvfb proof in the hub:
docs/assets/zeo-6.36-remote-button-2026-11-15/ (the order page, a move,
the status bar before and after, the written setting).
